"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"][9]={"university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M9-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M9-L1 – Motor, Load and Mechanical Operating Point<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M9 – Bachelor: Electrical Drives and Power Electronics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective area<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Overview of\nelectrical machines<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Power\nbalance<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Determine torque, speed, the\nmechanical load characteristic and efficiency using a DC machine model;\ndistinguish steady-state and dynamic operation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From the Electrical to the Mechanical Port<\/h2>\n<p>A drive consists of an energy source, a power converter, a machine\nand a mechanical load. When lifting a load, the machine must transfer\nenergy against gravity; when lowering it, the load can transfer energy\nto the machine. The direction of motion alone therefore does not\ndetermine whether the machine operates as a motor or a generator.<\/p>\n<p>We take the armature current\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas positive into the positive voltage terminal and the electromagnetic\ntorque\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the direction of positive angular velocity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ω<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a DC machine with constant excitation flux and idealised\ncommutation,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>M<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=Ri+L\\frac{di}{dt}+k_e\\omega,\\qquad M=k_t i.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The final voltage term is the back EMF (electromotive force). In\nconsistent SI units,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{V\\,s/rad}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{N\\,m/A}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhave the same numerical value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nprovided the conversion model is lossless:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_e\\omega i=M\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nMechanical power is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_\\mathrm{mech}=M\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nrotational speed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin revolutions per minute is converted using\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>60<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=2\\pi n/60<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt standstill, a motor can produce torque but cannot deliver mechanical\npower.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Load Characteristic and Steady-State Operating Point<\/h2>\n<p>For a rigid shaft with moment of inertia\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>J<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nviscous friction coefficient\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand external load torque\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M_L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nopposing rotation in the positive direction,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mover><mi>ω<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J\\dot\\omega=M-B\\omega-M_L.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With constant voltage and steady-state current,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M=k(u-k\\omega)/R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe motor characteristic decreases linearly with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ω<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe required load characteristic\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M_\\mathrm{erf}=M_L+B\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(subscript erf denotes required torque) increases for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTheir intersection is the steady-state operating point. If the shaft\nslows slightly at constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>u<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe motor torque increases in the steady-state electrical model,\ncounteracting the slowdown. Dynamic stability with finite\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmust be investigated separately.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_022_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Decreasing motor characteristic and increasing load characteristic intersecting at 200 rad/s and 0.8 Nm.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: In the steady-state motor–load model, the decreasing\nmotor-torque curve meets the increasing load-torque curve at 200 rad/s\nand 0.8 N·m.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>A fan may have an opposing torque that increases approximately with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(for positive rotation), whereas a conveyor belt often requires\napproximately constant load torque. The ideal no-load speed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u/k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalso assumes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe stall current\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u/R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis merely a model value and may be thermally unacceptable in\npractice.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example: Operating Point and Power<\/h2>\n<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>24<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=24\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=0.5\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the SI units above,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.001<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=0.001\\,\\mathrm{N\\,m\\,s/rad}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M_L=0.6\\,\\mathrm{N\\,m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k(u-k\\omega)/R=B\\omega+M_L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>k<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=\\frac{ku/R-M_L}{B+k^2/R}=200\\,\\mathrm{rad/s},\\qquad i=8\\,\\mathrm A.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This is approximately 1910 revolutions per minute. The\nelectromagnetic torque is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.8\\,\\mathrm{N\\,m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe power delivered to the external load is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>120<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_L=M_L\\omega=120\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe electrical input power is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>192<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">192\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the steady-state model,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>32<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">32\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis lost through\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><msup><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Ri^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">40\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthrough\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><msup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B\\omega^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe balance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>192<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>120<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>32<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">192=120+32+40<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis satisfied. Efficiency with respect to the external load is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>62.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">62.5\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe internal conversion power of 160 W must not be confused with useful\noutput power.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Operating Quadrants and Model Limitations<\/h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Angular velocity<\/th>\n<th>Electromagnetic torque<\/th>\n<th>Power conversion<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>positive<\/td>\n<td>positive<\/td>\n<td>Motoring<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>positive<\/td>\n<td>negative<\/td>\n<td>Generating braking<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>negative<\/td>\n<td>negative<\/td>\n<td>Reverse motoring<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>negative<\/td>\n<td>positive<\/td>\n<td>Reverse generating braking<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The table describes internal power conversion. Useful electrical\nregeneration requires the converted power to exceed the losses and both\nthe source and converter to be capable of absorbing energy. The DC\nmachine model cannot be transferred directly to induction machines or\nfield-oriented controlled synchronous machines. Saturation, brush\nvoltage drop and temperature-dependent resistance are not included\nhere.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does the operating point in the example change for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.81<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M_L=0.81\\,\\mathrm{N\\,m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4.8<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.81<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.021<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>190<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=(4.8-0.81)/0.021=190\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i=10\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M=1\\,\\mathrm{N\\,m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A motor produces a positive torque of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{N\\,m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=-50\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhat power is converted internally?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M\\omega=-100\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe machine absorbs mechanical energy; the net electrical output depends\non the losses and converter.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is the efficiency in the example zero when the shaft is locked,\nprovided positive input power is supplied?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_L=M_L\\omega=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nwithout motion, no external mechanical work is performed.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Further study:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Power\nsemiconductors and switching states<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.eti.kit.edu/studium_ems.php\">KIT – Electrical\ndrives and power electronics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Bachelor EAL, WS 25/26, course\n2306660, description: drives and conversion; not a mathematical\ntorque-speed model. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.tudelft.nl/course-lectures/4-dc-machines-and-drives/\">TU\nDelft OpenCourseWare, Electrical machines and drives, Lecture 4<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nLecture 04, topic list: armature voltage, torque, speed control; slides\nnot reviewed. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-685-electric-machines-fall-2013/pages/course-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 6.685 Electric Machines, course notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · (Chapter 6)\n· Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M9-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M9-L2 – Power Semiconductors and Switching States<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M9 – Bachelor: Electrical Drives and Power Electronics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective area<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Diodes and\nrectification<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Transistors<\/a>,\n<a href=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Energy\nstorage components<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Analyse the switching states of\nan inductive load and estimate conduction, switching and thermal losses\nusing a simplified model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Switching Rather than Linear Voltage Regulation<\/h2>\n<p>A linear control element can sustain a substantial voltage and carry\na substantial current at the same time. By contrast, an ideal power\nswitch has two states: in the conducting state the voltage drop is zero,\nand in the blocking state the current is zero. Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=ui<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis zero in both states. Energy flow can be controlled by varying the\nduration of the states. The switching frequency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_s=1/T_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes the repetition rate of a complete switching cycle.<\/p>\n<p>In the model, a diode conducts only in the forward direction; a\ncontrolled MOSFET can conduct current in both directions when its\nchannel is on. Its body diode can provide a current path even when the\ngate is off. An IGBT is often combined with an antiparallel diode. The\nsuitability of a device depends on blocking voltage, current, losses,\ntemperature and switching behaviour, not solely on the type of\ndevice.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Inductive Load and Freewheeling<\/h2>\n<p>For an inductor,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_L=L\\,di/dt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf the inductor current is positive, it cannot change abruptly at finite\nvoltage. When the upper switch of a buck converter is turned off, the\nfreewheeling diode initially carries the current. In the on state, the\nsource supplies energy to the load and, where applicable, to the\ninductor; during freewheeling, the inductor releases stored energy.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_023_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two switching states of the buck converter: source and inductor on the left, freewheeling path through the diode on the right.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: With the buck switch on, the source feeds the\ninductor and load; after switch-off, the freewheel diode allows inductor\ncurrent to continue.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>State<\/th>\n<th>Upper switch<\/th>\n<th>Freewheeling diode at positive current<\/th>\n<th>Node potential relative to ground (ideal)<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>On<\/td>\n<td>conducting<\/td>\n<td>blocking<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Off<\/td>\n<td>blocking<\/td>\n<td>conducting<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>When the inductor current ceases, freewheeling ends. A third state\nwith zero inductor current is then required. In a synchronous\nhalf-bridge, an actively controlled switch replaces the freewheeling\npath. Both switches must not be turned on simultaneously\n(shoot-through); dead time separates their drive signals. During dead\ntime, the node voltage depends on the current direction and diode path.\nReal inductive switching circuits also require protection against\nparasitic overvoltages. This module uses hypothetical circuits and\nsimulations only; it is not a construction guide.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Loss Models<\/h2>\n<p>For a MOSFET with assumed constant on-resistance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">,<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\mathrm{DS,on}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\naverage conduction loss is obtained from the mean square current over\nthe entire cycle. For approximately constant current\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand duty cycle\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">,<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msup><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_\\mathrm{leit}\\approx D R_\\mathrm{DS,on}I^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(the subscript leit denotes conduction). For a diode with constant\nforward voltage drop\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe approximate loss is instead\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><\/msub><mi>I<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_D=(1-D)U_F I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>Current and voltage overlap during real switching transitions. A\nsimple model with linear overlap gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mi>U<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\mathrm{sw}\\approx\\frac12 U I(t_r+t_f),\\qquad P_\\mathrm{sw}=f_s E_\\mathrm{sw}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrepresent the effective overlap durations. The equation assumes suitable\ncurrent and voltage waveforms; actual switching energies in data sheets\nmay differ. Gate drive, output capacitance and diode reverse recovery\ncontribute additional losses unless already included in the switching\nenergies used.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example: Loss and Temperature<\/h2>\n<p>A hypothetical buck converter operates with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>48<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=48\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=10\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_s=20\\,\\mathrm{kHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">,<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\mathrm{DS,on}=20\\,\\mathrm{m}\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_F=0.8\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_r+t_f=100\\,\\mathrm{ns}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_\\mathrm{leit}=1\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_D=4\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>24<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\mathrm{sw}=24\\,\\mathrm{\\mu J}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.48<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_\\mathrm{sw}=0.48\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTheir sum is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.48<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5.48\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>Assuming useful output power of 240 W and only these losses,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>240<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>240<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>5.48<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>97.77<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=240/(240+5.48)\\approx97.77\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis separate power balance assumes a correspondingly adjusted input\ncurrent. For the MOSFET alone, assuming total thermal resistance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\theta=25\\,\\mathrm{K/W}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto a constant ambient temperature of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_a=40\\,^\\circ\\mathrm C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>77<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_j\\approx T_a+R_\\theta(P_\\mathrm{leit}+P_\\mathrm{sw})=77\\,^\\circ\\mathrm C.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The diode requires its own thermal balance. Mutual heating is not\nincluded in the model. Demonstrating compliance with temperature limits\nrequires real data, cooling conditions and temperature dependencies.\nUnder constant assumptions, doubling the switching frequency doubles\nswitching losses, not conduction losses.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the sum of the three losses at 40 kHz with all other data\nunchanged?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.96<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5.96<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+4+0.96=5.96\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can an open switching device be exposed to high voltage when\nthere is no freewheeling path?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> A forced rapid change in current produces a\nhigh induced voltage according to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_L=L\\,di/dt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does doubling the current affect conduction loss at constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">,<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\mathrm{DS,on}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> MOSFET conduction loss increases by a factor\nof four. Diode loss doubles in the constant voltage drop model.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Further study:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">DC/DC\nconverters and pulse-width modulation<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-334-power-electronics-spring-2007/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 6.334 Power Electronics, lecture notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · (Chapters\n1, 5, 11 and 13) · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M9-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M9-L3 – DC/DC Converters and Pulse-Width Modulation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M9 – Bachelor: Electrical Drives and Power Electronics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective area<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Switching\nstates<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Capacitors\nand inductors<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive voltage conversion ratios\nfrom periodic energy-storage balances and check current ripple and the\nvalidity of continuous conduction.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Average Value and Periodic State<\/h2>\n<p>Pulse-width modulation (PWM) changes the duration\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">DT_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof the on state, with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfixed here. In periodic steady-state operation, the states of the\nstorage elements are identical at the beginning and end of each period.\nThis yields the volt-second and charge balances<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/msubsup><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/msubsup><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_0^{T_s}u_L(t)\\,dt=0,\\qquad \\int_0^{T_s}i_C(t)\\,dt=0.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The first relation follows from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L\\Delta i_L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe second from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C\\Delta u_C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThey also apply to non-sinusoidal signals. We consider idealised\nnon-isolated converters in continuous conduction mode (CCM) and assume\nsmall output-voltage ripple. The output voltage can then be regarded as\napproximately constant during one period.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Step-Down Converter (Buck)<\/h2>\n<p>The switching states from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M9-L2<\/a>\ngive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_L=U_d-U_o<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nduring the on state and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_L=-U_o<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nduring freewheeling. The volt-second balance gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D(U_d-U_o)+(1-D)(-U_o)=0,\\qquad U_o=D U_d.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In the idealised model, the inductor current rises and falls\nlinearly. The peak-to-peak ripple is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta i_{L,pp}=\\frac{(U_d-U_o)D}{Lf_s}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_024_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"PWM node voltage of 48/0 V and triangular inductor current from 3.4 to 4.6 A at D = 0.5.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: With 48 V input and duty cycle 0.5, the PWM\nswitching node alternates between 48 and 0 V; modelled inductor current\nswings between 3.4 and 4.6 A.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>Example:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>48<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_d=48\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>500<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=500\\,\\mathrm{\\mu H}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_s=20\\,\\mathrm{kHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_L=6\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>24<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_o=24\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_o=4\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta i_{L,pp}=1.2\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe minimum inductor current is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.4\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nCCM is thus consistent with the assumptions. An ideal converter delivers\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>96<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_o=96\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand draws an average of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat 48 V. The average input current is not identical to the average\ninductor current.<\/p>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=100\\,\\mathrm{\\mu F}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand an ideal capacitor, approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>o<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mi>C<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>75<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta u_{o,pp}=\\Delta i_{L,pp}/(8Cf_s)=75\\,\\mathrm{mV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe formula follows from the positive triangular area of the capacitor\ncurrent\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i_C=i_L-I_o<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nover half a period. Equivalent series resistance (ESR) of the capacitor\nproduces an additional ripple contribution.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Step-Up Converter (Boost)<\/h2>\n<p>When the lower switch is turned on,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis applied across the inductor; the diode to the output blocks and the\ncapacitor supplies the load. When the switch is turned off, the induced\nvoltage adds to the source voltage; the diode carries inductor current\nto the output. In CCM,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_L=U_d-U_o<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nduring the off state. Hence<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D U_d+(1-D)(U_d-U_o)=0,\\qquad U_o=\\frac{U_d}{1-D}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The ripple is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta i_{L,pp}=U_dD/(Lf_s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>24<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_d=24\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>500<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=500\\,\\mathrm{\\mu H}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_s=20\\,\\mathrm{kHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthis gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>48<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_o=48\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta i_{L,pp}=1.2\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a load of 24 Ω,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_o=2\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand, in the lossless model,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_d=4\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHere the inductor current is also the input current. CCM is satisfied.\nThe apparent increase in output voltage towards infinity as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D\\to1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a limitation of the model: without an off state, periodic energy\ndelivery through the diode cannot occur.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model Selection at the CCM Boundary<\/h2>\n<p>For a buck converter with a freewheeling diode and resistive load,\nthe CCM boundary occurs at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_o=\\Delta i_{L,pp}/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the first example, this is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.6\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe corresponding load resistance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>24<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.6<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\mathrm{krit}=24/0.6=40\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(subscript krit denotes the critical value). With a higher load\nresistance, the CCM model would predict negative current. The diode\nprevents this; a load-dependent model with a zero-current interval (DCM)\nthen applies. An actively bidirectional synchronous converter, by\ncontrast, can allow negative current.<\/p>\n<p>Switching ripple is not the same as the slower control error. An\naveraged converter model is often appropriate for control design;\ncurrent peaks, losses and electromagnetic interference require closer\nexamination of the switching waveforms.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor an ideal buck converter with 36 V input and 12 V output?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=1/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">At a constant operating point, how do current ripple and the ideal\naverage output voltage change if the inductance is doubled?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> Ripple is halved; the average remains\nunchanged as long as the CCM model applies.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">An ideal boost converter delivers 36 V from a 12 V input. Determine\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the average input current for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>o<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_o=1\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=2/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_d=3\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom the power balance. Whether CCM applies must still be checked using\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Further study:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Inverters and\nmachine power supply<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-334-power-electronics-spring-2007/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 6.334 Power Electronics, lecture notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · (Chapters 5\nand 8) · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M9-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M9-L4 – Inverters and Machine Supplies<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M9 – Bachelor: Electrical Drives and Power Electronics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Three-phase\nsystems<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Fourier\nanalysis<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">PWM<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain the output states of a\nfull bridge, calculate the fundamental and harmonics, and assess the\ninteraction between the converter and the machine.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Single-phase voltage-source full bridge<\/h2>\n<p>Two half bridges A and B are connected between the poles of an ideal\nDC link\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe load is connected between the midpoints. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∈<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_A,s_B\\in\\{0,1\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n1 denotes the upper switch being on and 0 the lower switch being on in\nthe respective leg. Dead times are initially neglected. The load voltage\nis the difference between the two node potentials:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_{AB}=U_d(s_A-s_B).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_{AB}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+U_d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-U_d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The zero states do not mean that no load current flows. An inductive\nload can continue to carry current during these states. In either half\nbridge, simultaneous conduction of both switches is prohibited.\nDepending on the circuit, the antiparallel diodes or conducting channels\nallow current in both directions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Square-wave operation and harmonics<\/h2>\n<p>In symmetrical square-wave operation,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_{AB}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalternates between\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+U_d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-U_d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nafter each half of the fundamental period. For a waveform starting at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith a positive value, the Fourier series is (except at\ndiscontinuities)<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><mi>π<\/mi><\/mfrac><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><\/mrow><\/munder><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>h<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_{AB}(t)=\\frac{4U_d}{\\pi}\\sum_{h=1,3,5,\\ldots}\\frac{\\sin(h\\omega_1t)}{h}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The fundamental has an RMS value of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_1=4U_d/(\\pi\\sqrt{2})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhile the entire square wave has an RMS value of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe total harmonic distortion of the voltage is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>U<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>−<\/mo><msubsup><mi>U<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>48.34<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{THD}=\\sqrt{U_d^2-U_1^2}/U_1\\approx48.34\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA higher total RMS value does not imply a higher usable fundamental\ncomponent.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_025_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Square-wave load voltage of the full bridge and its normalised spectrum of odd harmonics.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The idealised full bridge produces a bipolar\nsquare-wave voltage; its normalised spectrum contains odd harmonics of\ndecreasing amplitude.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>For an ideal RL load,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mi>L<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h=U_h/\\sqrt{R^2+(h\\omega_1 L)^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\napplies to each harmonic. The inductance attenuates higher-order\nharmonic currents. Example:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>48<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_d=48\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_1=50\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=4\\,\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=20\\,\\mathrm{mH}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe results are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>43.22<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_1\\approx43.22\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>5.80<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_1\\approx5.80\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>14.41<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_3\\approx14.41\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_3\\approx0.75\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(all RMS values). This is a passive model load, not yet a complete\nelectrical machine with back EMF.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sinusoidal PWM and three-phase operation<\/h2>\n<p>In bipolar sinusoidal PWM of a full bridge, a sinusoidal reference\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m\\sin(\\omega_1t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis compared with a normalised triangular carrier. In the linear range\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le m\\le1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand at a high carrier frequency, the local average of the load voltage\nis approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">mU_d\\sin(\\omega_1t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe fundamental amplitude is approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">mU_d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHarmonic components occur, among other places, in groups around the\ncarrier frequency and its multiples. The switching voltage itself\nremains pulsed.<\/p>\n<p>A two-level three-phase inverter has three half bridges. With\nsinusoidal references displaced by 120 degrees, the same carrier-based\nmodulation in its linear range yields a fundamental line-to-line voltage\nwith an RMS value of<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msqrt><mn>3<\/mn><\/msqrt><mi>m<\/mi><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_{LL,1}\\approx\\frac{\\sqrt{3}mU_d}{2\\sqrt{2}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>300<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_d=300\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe model gives approximately 147 V line-to-line RMS. The factor 1/2\narises here from the individual half bridge. It must not be erroneously\nintroduced into the single-phase full-bridge formula. The fundamental\nlimit depends on the modulation method; space-vector modulation can\nutilise the DC link differently.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Effects on the machine<\/h2>\n<p>In an AC machine, frequency and fundamental voltage affect flux,\ntorque and rotational speed. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npole pairs, a synchronous rotating field has a speed of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_s=60f_1/p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin revolutions per minute. In an induction machine, the mechanical speed\nis slightly lower in normal motoring operation. The difference is\ndescribed by slip. Simple V/f control keeps the fundamental voltage\napproximately proportional to frequency in the intermediate frequency\nrange to maintain an approximately constant magnetic flux. At low\nfrequencies, the resistive voltage drop becomes important. Once the\nvoltage limit is reached, field weakening is often necessary. This is\nnot a complete torque control method.<\/p>\n<p>Pulsed voltages can cause additional copper and iron losses, torque\nripple and insulation stress. In generating operation, the DC link must\nbe able to absorb the returning energy or dissipate it in a controlled\nmanner. A constant DC-link voltage in the model explicitly assumes that\nthis energy can be absorbed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">An ideal full bridge with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_d=60\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\noperates with bipolar sinusoidal modulation at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.7<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=0.7<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhat is the approximate RMS value of the fundamental?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>42<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>29.7<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">42/\\sqrt{2}\\approx29.7\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A rotating field is produced at 50 Hz in a machine with two pole\npairs. What is the synchronous speed?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1500<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1500\\,\\mathrm{min^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is the current in an inductive load often less distorted than\nthe switching voltage?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> The inductive reactance increases with\nfrequency and thus limits higher-order harmonic currents.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Further reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Simulation of\na drive system<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-334-power-electronics-spring-2007/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 6.334 Power Electronics, lecture notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · (Chapters\n3, 9 and 15) · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-685-electric-machines-fall-2013/pages/course-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 6.685 Electric Machines, course notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · (Chapters 4,\n8 and 10) · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M9-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M9-L5 – Simulation of a Drive System<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M9 – Bachelor: Electrical Drives and Power Electronics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Motor\noperating point<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">PWM<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;page=study-path&amp;section=S9&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6 –\nMeasurement and Introduction to Control<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Model\nvalidation<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Simulate a coupled electrical\nand mechanical model with a load step, compare open-loop control with PI\nfeedback control, and check the energy balance and dependence on step\nsize.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Averaged model and experimental question<\/h2>\n<p>The switching period of a converter can be much shorter than the\nmechanical response time. In the specified positive-current range, we\nreplace an ideal synchronous buck converter with the average motor\nvoltage\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=D U_d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le D\\le1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe DC motor from M9-L1 is described by the coupled differential\nequations<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mover><mi>i<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>J<\/mi><mover><mi>ω<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L\\dot i=u-Ri-k\\omega,\\qquad J\\dot\\omega=ki-B\\omega-M_L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In this approximation, the converter has no losses of its own or\nadditional energy storage elements. Current and speed dynamics are\nretained, but PWM ripple is not. We investigate a step in the external\nload torque, starting from a known steady state. The parameters are\nfictitious and do not describe a particular commercial product.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Parameter<\/th>\n<th>Value<\/th>\n<th>Parameter<\/th>\n<th>Value<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>24 V<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>0.5 Ω<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>20 mH<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>J<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>0.02 kg m²<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>0.1 Nm/A or Vs/rad<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>0.001 Nms/rad<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M_L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbefore/after 0.5 s<\/td>\n<td>0.4 / 0.8 Nm<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_\\mathrm{soll}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>150 rad/s<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>In the initial state,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>150<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i=(0.4+B\\cdot150)/k=5.5\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>17.75<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=Ri+k\\omega=17.75\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWithout feedback control, this manipulated variable remains constant.\nAfter the load step, the steady-state calculation predicts\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>k<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>131.36<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_\\infty=(ku/R-0.8)/(B+k^2/R)\\approx131.36\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith ideal speed control at 150 rad/s, however,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i=9.5\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>19.75<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=19.75\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare required. Both voltages lie within the model limit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>PI control and saturation<\/h2>\n<p>We compare open-loop voltage control with proportional-integral speed\ncontrol. For the error\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>e<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e=\\omega_\\mathrm{soll}-\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(soll denotes the setpoint) and the integrator state\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe have<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/msub><mi>e<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mover><mi>z<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><\/msub><mi>e<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">clip<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u^*=K_Pe+z,\\qquad \\dot z=K_Ie,\\qquad u=\\operatorname{clip}(u^*,0,U_d).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_P=0.12\\,\\mathrm{V\\,s/rad}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_I=0.6\\,\\mathrm{V/rad}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare model parameters; the integrator is initialised at 17.75 V. The\nsimulation thus starts without an artificial voltage step. During\nsaturation, integration continues only if the error drives the\nunconstrained manipulated variable back into the permitted range\n(conditional integration to prevent windup). The controller assumes\nideal speed measurement. A current limit and an inner current control\nloop would be typical additional requirements for real drives.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reproducible Python experiment<\/h2>\n<p>The states are advanced simultaneously from the values at the\nrespective time step using the explicit Euler method. All energy terms\nuse the same old state values and the same step size; the residual\nbalance serves as a numerical check.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> simulate(h, regulated):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    R, L, k, J, B <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.5<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">0.02<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">0.1<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">0.02<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">0.001<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    i, w, z <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">5.5<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">150.0<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">17.75<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    energy_in <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> energy_out <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.0<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    storage0 <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> L<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>i<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>i<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span> <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> J<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>w<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>w<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    min_w, max_i <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> w, i<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> n <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span>h)):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        t <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> n<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>h<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        load <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.4<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> t <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.5<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.8<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        error <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">150.0<\/span> <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> w<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        raw <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> z <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.12<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>error <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> regulated <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">17.75<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        u <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(<span class=\"fl\">0.0<\/span>, <span class=\"bu\">min<\/span>(<span class=\"fl\">24.0<\/span>, raw))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> regulated <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span> <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> raw <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">24<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">or<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>                          raw <span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">24<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> error <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">or<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>                          raw <span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> error <span class=\"op\">&gt;<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            z <span class=\"op\">+=<\/span> h<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"fl\">0.6<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>error<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        di <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (u <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> R<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>i <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> k<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>w)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>L<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        dw <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (k<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>i <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> B<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>w <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> load)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>J<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        energy_in <span class=\"op\">+=<\/span> h<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>u<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>i<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-21\"><a href=\"#cb1-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        energy_out <span class=\"op\">+=<\/span> h<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(R<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>i<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>i <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> B<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>w<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>w <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> load<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>w)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-22\"><a href=\"#cb1-22\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        i <span class=\"op\">+=<\/span> h<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>di<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-23\"><a href=\"#cb1-23\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        w <span class=\"op\">+=<\/span> h<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>dw<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-24\"><a href=\"#cb1-24\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        min_w, max_i <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">min<\/span>(min_w, w), <span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(max_i, i)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-25\"><a href=\"#cb1-25\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    storage1 <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> L<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>i<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>i<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span> <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> J<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>w<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>w<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-26\"><a href=\"#cb1-26\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    residual <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> energy_in <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> energy_out <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> (storage1<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>storage0)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-27\"><a href=\"#cb1-27\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> w, i, min_w, max_i, residual<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-28\"><a href=\"#cb1-28\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-29\"><a href=\"#cb1-29\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-30\"><a href=\"#cb1-30\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> h <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> (<span class=\"fl\">0.001<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">0.0005<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">0.00025<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-31\"><a href=\"#cb1-31\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> regulated <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> (<span class=\"va\">False<\/span>, <span class=\"va\">True<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-32\"><a href=\"#cb1-32\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        values <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> simulate(h, regulated)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-33\"><a href=\"#cb1-33\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(h, regulated, <span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(x, <span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> x <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> values))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-15\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-21\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-21\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-21\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-21\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Case,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.0005<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=0.0005\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega(4\\,\\mathrm s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin rad/s<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i(4\\,\\mathrm s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin A<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Minimum speed in rad/s<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Maximum current in A<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Open-loop<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">131.364<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">9.223<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">131.364<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">9.223<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PI<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">149.795<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">9.567<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">144.980<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">10.989<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_026_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Speed response after the load step at 0.5 s: open-loop control and PI control compared with the setpoint of 150 rad/s.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: After a load step at 0.5 s, speed decreases in both\ncases; the modelled PI controller returns it close to 150 rad/s while\nopen-loop control remains lower.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The PI controller limits the drop in speed and approaches the\nsetpoint. It can require a higher peak current than open-loop control.\nAfter four seconds, the current in the PI case is still slightly above\nits steady-state target. A snapshot does not establish that all states\nhave already reached steady state.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Energy balance, convergence and acceptance<\/h2>\n<p>Multiplying the electrical equation by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the mechanical equation by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ω<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe conversion terms\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ki\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncancel in the sum:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><msup><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><msup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mi>d<\/mi><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mi>L<\/mi><msup><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mi>J<\/mi><msup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ui=Ri^2+B\\omega^2+M_L\\omega+\n\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac12Li^2+\\frac12J\\omega^2\\right).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>After integration, this means that input energy minus losses minus\nload work minus the change in stored energy should equal zero. With\nexplicit Euler integration, a small numerical balance residual arises.\nThe following table gives its magnitude for the model.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Step size in ms<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Open-loop: residual magnitude in J<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">PI: residual magnitude in J<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">PI: speed at 4 s in rad/s<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.002057<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.001518<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">149.794389<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.5<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.001028<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.000757<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">149.794906<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.25<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.000514<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.000378<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">149.795165<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The approximately halved residual magnitudes are consistent with the\nfirst-order method used. The final speeds converge towards one another.\nA small balance residual checks the discrete calculation, but does not\nprove that the machine model is realistic.<\/p>\n<p>We choose the following reproducible acceptance criteria for this\nmodelling task: final speed error in the PI case no greater than 0.3\nrad/s; maximum current in the PI case below 12 A; magnitude of the\nenergy residual below 0.01 J; final speed difference between the two\nfiner step sizes below 0.01 rad/s. All four criteria are met for the\nstated test cases. The 12 A value is a fictitious test threshold, not an\nimplemented current limit or a permissible component load. Even a passed\ntest does not justify immediate hardware construction. Switching losses,\ntemperature, sensor delay, current limiting and the real mechanical load\nmust be modelled and checked separately.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Determine the steady-state manipulated voltage at 150 rad/s and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M_L=1.6\\,\\mathrm{N\\,m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIs it achievable in the model?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1.6<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.15<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>17.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i=(1.6+0.15)/0.1=17.5\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>17.5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>23.75<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=0.5\\cdot17.5+15=23.75\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe voltage is sufficient in the model, but the fictitious 12 A\nthreshold is exceeded in steady state.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How much mechanical energy is stored at 150 rad/s?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><msup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>225<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J\\omega^2/2=225\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Extend the experiment to include a load step to 2 Nm. Can the\ncontroller achieve 150 rad/s in steady state?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>21.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i=21.5\\,\\mathrm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>25.75<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=25.75\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwould be required. This exceeds 24 V. At saturation, the modelled\nsteady-state speed is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4.8<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.021<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>133.33<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(4.8-2)/0.021\\approx133.33\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe integrator must not grow without bound.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Completion:<\/strong> Record the operating point, converter\nassumptions, load step, numerical balance and at least one model\nlimitation in a brief model description. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;page=study-path&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Study\npathway<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-685-electric-machines-fall-2013/pages/course-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 6.685 Electric Machines, course notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · (Chapter 6)\n· Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-334-power-electronics-spring-2007/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare, 6.334 Power Electronics, lecture notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · (Chapter 8)\n· Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://fbswiki.org/wiki/index.php/Feedback_Systems%3A_An_Introduction_for_Scientists_and_Engineers\">Åström/Murray\n– Feedback Systems, Second Edition<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter index 3, 5, 6, 9 and\n11: modeling, dynamics, linear systems, transfer functions and PID;\nlinked chapters not re-reviewed. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
